\subsection{Parallelizing pixel maths}
\subsubsection{Using CUDA to parallelize tasks}
If we want to parallelize the execution of estimation on CUDA, we need to ensure that the GPU has access to the data it needs to perform calculations on. In the first iteration of motion estimation, we blindly allocated memory inside the \code{c63_motion_estimate} for both the inputs and the outputs, and synchronized the execution at the end of this function. This was a bad idea, and in hindsight it makes complete sense. The total time for encoding went up to circa 540 seconds, even with parallelization!

Our failed attempt quickly made us realize that we would benefit from reusing memory allocations, so we decided to allocate pinned memory for all our transfers before starting to encode a single frame (see \commit{f038f37ca7babe508400ba9045d74d26c6f2aafd}).

We also thought that reducing the amount of data-which-wasn't-going-to-be-used-anyway we transferred to the device would be a good idea. Additionally, because the GPU is not capable of reading host memory directly (without using mapped memory), we decided to get rid of any unnecessary indirection from the parameters sent to the kernel functions (see \commit{0581de573f7c0c71123f6a185d969055ab63231e}).

\subsubsection{Parallelizing estimation}
After having set up memory on the device, and having dealt with the required wrappers for the kernel, we can calculate estimation in parallel on the GPU instead (see \commit{8bc5079bd962fe9c7d2c7120442072aa5cb42f54} and \commit{d30b1aa11223f4b4a18fe1e1939b0ab37fd6ef79}). While simply moving the kernel code to run on the GPU by itself doesn't improve performance much, getting rid of loops inside each kernel does drastically speed up the execution.

As a note, we can avoid passing the index locations to the device, too, since each thread is aware of its own CUDA thread index, which we can treat as a free parameter. 

This parallelization, with no further changes to the flow, reduces the total time from 122 \secs\, to roughly 28 \secs. Huge!

\subsubsection{Avoiding synchronization}
Looking at the profiling results of the preivous steps, at 28 seconds, we saw that synchronization of devices was slow. In order to improve this, we decided to run each kernel on a stream, and only sync them afterwards. On its own, this improved the runtime by a smidge, but nothing meaningful; but it did permit us to reorder the synchronization, and only do it when required.

We would extend this asynchronous execution to memory transfers between the host and the device too, which again didn't help much on its own, but actually set us up to interleave certain CPU function calls. It was a this point where we realized we could benefit from prefetching device memory as soon as the old device memory was no longer going to be used. Remember: \code{dct_quantize}, \code{dequantize_dct} and \code{write_frame} entirely use host memory, and are the last steps of each frame loop. Effectively, this means we can start loading in the next buffer to the device prior to calling these slow CPU functions asynchronously.

Prefetching the next frame by the use of a framebuffer (see \commit{0a8ef5a568f3429aac6312a84c212801112f8ae7}) reduced the total execution time from roughly 28 seconds to around 17 seconds. Again, huge!

\subsubsection{Parallelizing compensation}
After our success with parallelizing estimation, we decided we could do the same thing with compensation. We hadn't done this prior, since it wasn't really the bottleneck for performance until this point.

In our first iteration of the code, we would keep the CPU-version of this around, since the decoder also depended on this, and we didn't want to deal with refactoring the decoder yet. Eventually, we would just upgrade the decoder to support the required initialization for CUDA, since it made the code more maintainable regardless, plus it really had no downsides.

One of the benefits of running compensation on the GPU is that we no longer had to wait for the device memory of the motion vectors to return to the host, since we can now read from the same device memory addresses the estimation would write to previously. This would effectively parallelize motion compensation with the memory transfer of the motion vectors.

We didn't really observe much of a difference after optimizing compensation, albeit the kernel itself would run faster. The bottleneck at this point is mostly bandwidth transfer anyway.

\subsection{Tuning kernel code}
Even if the bottleneck at this point seems to lay at the bandwidth for memory transfer, we decided to look into making each kernel run faster on its own. Upgrading hardware could make these optimizations relevant, even if isn't showing any major benefit for the particular hardware we are testing on.

\subsection{Sum of Absolute Differences (SAD)}
Motion estimation is calculated for each macroblock (a non-overlapping 8x8 region of a frame) in the current frame. This is done by finding the best match for each macroblock in the current frame with the one in the previous frame. The best match, in this case, is the macroblock with the minimal total pixel difference.

We can make the calculation of the SAD quicker by having each thread in the warp calculate the difference between a single pair of pixels, and sum up the differences after all 64 pairs have been calculated. This makes no thread left hanging, as each warp contains 32 threads \cite{nvidia_cuda_best_practices}.

\subsection{Improving macroblock loops}
To optimize the traversal of macroblocks and minimize memory contention, we distribute the workload across threads using a stride of \code{blockDim.x} and \code{blockDim.y}. This allows each thread to process different candidate positions in parallel, without blocking memory, improving overall efficiency. 

Additionally, using \code{atomicMin} instead of synchronizing threads each time someone wants to write their value avoids having to wait for all threads to finish.

However, further optimization could be achieved by improving memory access patterns to ensure better coalescence and warp utilization.

\subsection{Shared memory and constant memory}
In order to avoid waiting for data transfer between warps, we make use of shared memory for the SAD blocks. Shared memory is much faster than the global memory it would have used otherwise. We also initialize constant memory since they are never changed, and can thus be cached between kernel launches.


